# OTS (sync, 2026-10-29): the recipe's literal added tokens (patch 0116, tensorfold/cuda/literal_tokens.py, new on the
# synced base; production v110-ots124 has no masking) cost every long chat request host time production does not pay,
# and took masked prompts around OTS 0110's prompt ids. Measured on a ~668k-token agent conversation (workstation CPU,
# scripts/glm53/v110/perpatch/bench_literal.py): mask() 55 ms a request, 51 of them a Python loop over every character
# (_collides); a request holding any "\u" escape - common in code and JSON tool results - was flagged masked even when
# no guarded token decoded from it, and a masked prompt was encoded whole: 646 ms a turn, where 0110 tokenizes the
# tail in ~10 ms. Three changes, every id unchanged:
#   - _collides is one regex over the masks' private-use range (the same answer: tests/host/test_literal_fast.py
#     against the recipe's loop on 4,003 texts with the range's edges): mask() 55 -> 9 ms;
#   - mask() hands the request back unmasked when masking changed nothing (no mask character in the result: the masked
#     values equal the request's, so it renders the same text, which LiteralTokens.encode and a plain encode turn into
#     the same ids): such a turn goes through 0110's prompt ids;
#   - a masked prompt goes through prompt ids of its own (GlmApp.literal_prompt_ids, LiteralTokens.encode the encoder;
#     off with TF_GLM_PROMPT_IDS=0 like the plain one): the cut rule stays exact, because LiteralTokens.encode encodes
#     the text between masks piece by piece, the tokenizer splits each piece at its added tokens, and a cut falls only
#     at an added token's start (a mask is one character). tests/host/test_prompt_ids.py C checks every masked prompt's
#     ids and Sampling against the uncached path and bounds the literal cache's tokenized share.
# Applies after glm-host-fixes-ported.patch (its _prompt_ids and app.py).
